WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:01.510
Philip, brace yourself.

00:00:01.510 --> 00:00:04.210
I’m about to say something controversial...

00:00:04.210 --> 00:00:05.210
What is it?

00:00:05.210 --> 00:00:06.210
The earth is flat?

00:00:06.210 --> 00:00:07.890
Tomatoes aren’t really a fruit?

00:00:07.890 --> 00:00:11.510
The Star Wars Prequels were better than the
Originals?

00:00:11.510 --> 00:00:14.080
Renting isn’t always a bad financial decision...

00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:15.429
Say what?

00:00:15.429 --> 00:00:18.109
But isn’t renting just throwing money away?

00:00:18.109 --> 00:00:22.380
When you buy a home, your monthly payments
are going towards something you will eventually own.

00:00:22.540 --> 00:00:24.040
Yes, but not for a long time.

00:00:24.050 --> 00:00:29.039
Thanks to amortization, the first 5 to 10
years of mortgage payments are mostly interest.

00:00:29.039 --> 00:00:31.529
But… but the home is appreciating, right?

00:00:31.529 --> 00:00:35.629
So even if you’re not paying down the principal,
you’re still making money, right?

00:00:35.629 --> 00:00:38.670
True, but not that much more than inflation.

00:00:38.670 --> 00:00:40.739
Homeownership might work out for some, but

00:00:40.739 --> 00:00:43.050
can become a financial burden for others.

00:00:43.050 --> 00:00:45.920
So when is renting a good financial decision?

00:00:45.920 --> 00:00:49.000
And is renting viable as a long-term strategy?

00:00:58.059 --> 00:00:59.140
This is Kira.

00:00:59.140 --> 00:01:04.140
She just landed a job in a new city, and has
already started shopping for places to live.

00:01:04.360 --> 00:01:07.800
She’s trying to decide if she should take
the money she’s saved up and put it toward

00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:12.560
a house, or invest it in index funds
while she rents.

00:01:13.960 --> 00:01:18.140
A great way to find out whether you should
rent or buy is to look at the Price-to-Rent

00:01:18.140 --> 00:01:19.520
ratio.

00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:24.369
Find two similar houses, apartments, or condos
in your area; one for sale and one for rent.

00:01:24.369 --> 00:01:28.590
Divide the sale price of one by the annual
rental cost of the other.

00:01:28.590 --> 00:01:33.979
Experts say if the P/R ratio is over
15, it’s probably less expensive to rent.

00:01:33.979 --> 00:01:39.450
For example, the P/R ratio for the city of
Cincinnati is about 8.3, according to the

00:01:39.450 --> 00:01:40.789
U.S. Census Bureau.

00:01:40.789 --> 00:01:43.409
In our hometown of Austin, it’s 22.9.

00:01:43.409 --> 00:01:47.009
And in San Francisco, it’s 50.1!

00:01:47.009 --> 00:01:52.899
Put another way, a $600,000 home in San Francisco
would rent for only $1,000 a month!

00:01:52.899 --> 00:01:57.740
There are even free “Rent vs. Buy” calculators
online that will help you do the math with

00:01:57.740 --> 00:01:59.060
your own specifics!

00:01:59.060 --> 00:02:02.750
After shopping around, Kira finds an area
that she’s happy with.

00:02:02.750 --> 00:02:05.660
The average home price is $270,000.

00:02:05.660 --> 00:02:08.500
And the average rent is $1,100 a month.

00:02:08.500 --> 00:02:14.300
That means her area’s P/R ratio is 20.5,
and she’ll get more bang for her buck by

00:02:14.300 --> 00:02:15.300
renting.

00:02:15.300 --> 00:02:18.560
She also doesn’t know if she wants to live
in this city long term, making renting an

00:02:18.560 --> 00:02:20.830
even more attractive option.

00:02:20.830 --> 00:02:25.030
She decides against buying a home, and will
invest the money she saved for a down payment

00:02:25.030 --> 00:02:28.180
in an index fund instead.

00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:33.560
When it comes to affording a place to live,
millennials have an uphill battle.

00:02:33.570 --> 00:02:37.330
Baby Boomers spent an average of 36% of their
income on rent.

00:02:37.330 --> 00:02:39.010
Gen-X spent 41%.

00:02:39.010 --> 00:02:45.980
And millennials now pay over 45% of their
pre-tax income on housing costs alone!

00:02:45.980 --> 00:02:50.510
This makes it especially hard to invest, save,
and give to charity.

00:02:50.510 --> 00:02:55.730
Experts suggest keeping your total housing
costs below 30% of your income.

00:02:55.730 --> 00:02:58.290
Kira’s new salary is $58,000 a year.

00:02:58.290 --> 00:03:05.260
That would mean her maximum fixed housing
expense should ideally fall below $1450 a month.

00:03:05.440 --> 00:03:09.820
Since utilities are usually 20% of housing
expense, this would equate to $1160 a month

00:03:09.820 --> 00:03:13.360
in rent and $290 for utilities.

00:03:13.360 --> 00:03:16.920
Now she has a budget to start shopping for
her new place.

00:03:18.220 --> 00:03:20.680
Kira’s new job is downtown.

00:03:20.680 --> 00:03:22.800
But rents in that area are pretty high.

00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:26.180
Further out of town, costs are lower… but
then there’s the commute.

00:03:26.180 --> 00:03:33.200
One study found that every mile she has to
drive to work costs $795 a year through depreciation,

00:03:33.200 --> 00:03:35.980
gas, maintenance, and lost wages.

00:03:35.980 --> 00:03:40.600
For Kira, a 10 mile commute might cost her
$8,000 a year!

00:03:40.600 --> 00:03:44.870
She makes a mental note to add public transit
and carpool meetups to her list of search

00:03:44.870 --> 00:03:45.900
criteria.

00:03:45.900 --> 00:03:50.890
One often overlooked resource for renters
in a new city is to work with a licensed realtor.

00:03:50.890 --> 00:03:53.650
That’s right, they’re not just for home
buyers!

00:03:53.650 --> 00:03:57.050
A good Realtor will know the best areas to
begin your search.

00:03:57.050 --> 00:04:00.790
They might know about better deals that aren’t
widely advertised.

00:04:00.790 --> 00:04:04.880
And best of all, their commission is paid
by the property owner, not you!

00:04:04.880 --> 00:04:08.640
Kira would be smart to engage with one to
help narrow her search.

00:04:10.330 --> 00:04:15.080
Kira’s starting to get a little frustrated
because none of the areas of town she likes

00:04:15.090 --> 00:04:16.420
fall within her budget.

00:04:16.420 --> 00:04:21.489
She’s momentarily tempted to throw her budget
out the window, when a thought occurs to her.

00:04:21.489 --> 00:04:26.620
Maybe she can keep her housing costs in line
and live in a great area, if she will consider

00:04:26.620 --> 00:04:28.340
living with a roommate.

00:04:28.340 --> 00:04:32.680
30% of working age adults lived in so called
“doubled-up” households.

00:04:32.680 --> 00:04:36.270
She could rent out a spare room to offset
the higher costs!

00:04:36.270 --> 00:04:41.060
And if she wants to cut costs even more, she
might even look at “homeshare” networks.

00:04:41.060 --> 00:04:48.090
For example, Senior Homeshare or Golden Girls
Network pair younger renters with aging homeowners.

00:04:48.090 --> 00:04:53.860
Rent is highly reduced in exchange for a little
help around the house or rides to a doctor’s appointment.

00:04:55.509 --> 00:05:00.449
Another common pitfall renters face is failing
to closely read their lease terms.

00:05:00.449 --> 00:05:04.879
A lease is a legally enforceable document
and it’s your job to arm yourself with the

00:05:04.879 --> 00:05:07.129
knowledge of exactly what it says.

00:05:07.129 --> 00:05:11.930
For example, if you move out early and don’t
abide by the lease-breaking rules perfectly,

00:05:11.930 --> 00:05:15.289
you could be liable for the rent of the entirety
of the lease!

00:05:15.289 --> 00:05:20.169
And if you plan to sublet or rent out the
apartment, terms will be covered here too.

00:05:20.169 --> 00:05:24.561
Some people are surprised to find out that
damage of their belongings is generally not

00:05:24.561 --> 00:05:25.960
covered by their property owner.

00:05:25.960 --> 00:05:30.150
But renter’s insurance is designed to protect
against just that.

00:05:30.150 --> 00:05:35.469
In the event of water damage, fire, smoke,
or even theft, she can rest easy knowing her

00:05:35.469 --> 00:05:36.759
belongings are safe.

00:05:36.759 --> 00:05:37.840
The best part?

00:05:37.840 --> 00:05:40.040
Renter’s insurance is quite cheap.

00:05:40.040 --> 00:05:45.039
For the price of a pizza, Kira can know that
her possessions will be fine, even if her

00:05:45.039 --> 00:05:46.039
building is not.

00:05:46.039 --> 00:05:49.090
Kira is relieved to have found a place at
last.

00:05:49.090 --> 00:05:54.069
And because she has that index fund, she knows
she’ll keep pace with her homeowner friends.

00:05:54.069 --> 00:05:57.139
Renting may be the right choice for Kira,
but it’s not for everyone.

00:05:57.139 --> 00:06:01.029
If you’re not strategic about it, it can
feel like throwing money away.

00:06:01.029 --> 00:06:05.770
But if you’re careful to keep housing costs
low, and invest the money you save, it can

00:06:05.770 --> 00:06:08.419
be a viable long-term alternative to owning.

00:06:08.420 --> 00:06:10.380
And that’s our two cents!

00:06:11.020 --> 00:06:13.700
What's the P/R ratio in your neighborhood?

00:06:13.860 --> 00:06:16.180
Calculate it and share it with us in the comments!

